{"name":"Summer House","hex":"#c8efe2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"summer house","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of summerhouse."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/summer_house"}],"entries":[{"title":"Summer house","blocks":[{"text":"A summer house or summerhouse is a building or shelter used for relaxation in warm weather. This would often take the form of a small, roofed building on the grounds of a larger one, but could also be built in a garden or park, often designed to provide cool shady places of relaxation or retreat from the summer heat. It can also refer to a second residence, usually located in the country, that provides a cool and relaxing home to live in during the summer, such as a vacation property."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/ER-Burgberg-1-summerhouse-angle.jpg/330px-ER-Burgberg-1-summerhouse-angle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer_house"},{"title":"Summer House (2017 TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Summer House is an American reality television series that premiered on January 9, 2017, on Bravo. The series follows a group of friends who share a summer house in the Hamptons town of Southampton, New York. The friends work in the city during the week and travel to the Hamptons on the weekends."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/41/Summer_House_bravo_tv_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer_House_(2017_TV_series)"},{"title":"Summer","blocks":[{"text":"Summer or summertime is the hottest and brightest of the four temperate seasons, occurring after spring and before autumn. Starting on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. In the Northern Hemisphere, summer is usually defined as starting on June 21 or 22, and ending on September 22 or 23; and in the Southern Hemisphere, as the period between December 22 or 23 and March 20 or 21."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer"},{"title":"House","blocks":[{"text":"A house is a residential building. It may range in complexity from a rudimentary hut to a complex structure of wood, masonry, concrete or other material, outfitted with plumbing, electrical, and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning systems. Houses use a range of different roofing systems to keep precipitation such as rain from getting into the dwelling space. Houses generally have doors or locks to secure the dwelling space and protect its inhabitants and contents from burglars or other trespassers. Most conventional modern houses in Western cultures will contain one or more bedrooms and bathrooms, a kitchen or cooking area, and a living room. A house may have a separate dining room, or the eating area may be integrated into the kitchen or another room. Some large houses in North America have a recreation room. In traditional agriculture-oriented societies, domestic animals such as chickens or larger livestock may share part of the house with humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg/330px-Katsura_Imperial_Villa_in_Spring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg/500px-Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg/1280px-Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg/500px-Swedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Summerhouse (\"sommarstuga\") of the Swedish scientist, philosopher, and theologian Emanuel Swedenborg in the open-air museum Skansen in Stockholm, Sweden.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASwedenborgs_lusthus_Skansen_2005-07-29_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg/500px-Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg/1280px-Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg/500px-Namdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Norwegian \"hytte\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANamdalseid-cottage-nystua.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Mummonm%C3%B6kki.jpg/500px-Mummonm%C3%B6kki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Mummonm%C3%B6kki.jpg/500px-Mummonm%C3%B6kki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Finnish \"kesämökki\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMummonm%C3%B6kki.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%2